\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Base model.}
We evaluate \rasp{} on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{deepseek2025r1}, a 7B-parameter reasoning model built on the Qwen2.5 architecture~\citep{qwen2.5} with 28 transformer layers.
The model uses Grouped Query Attention (GQA;~\citealp{ainslie2023gqa}) with 28 query heads and 4 key-value heads per layer (group size $G{=}7$), and an MLP intermediate dimension of 18{,}944.
As a reasoning-distilled model whose chain-of-thought capabilities originate from reinforcement learning, it provides a direct testbed for studying how structural pruning interacts with RLVR-based recovery.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We evaluate on GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}, a benchmark of 1{,}319 grade-school math problems requiring multi-step arithmetic reasoning.
We report exact-match accuracy: the final numerical answer is extracted from the model's generation and compared against the ground truth.
For rapid iteration during development, we use a fixed 100-problem subset ($N{=}100$); final results use the full test set ($N{=}1{,}319$).
We define recovery $\Delta$ as the accuracy gain from post-prune to post-recovery, capturing how much reasoning ability the recovery pipeline restores independent of initial pruning damage.

\paragraph{Hardware.}
All experiments run on a single node with 2$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (94\,GB VRAM each).
The two pruning variants (Standard and \rasp{}) are trained in parallel on separate GPUs to ensure identical wall-clock conditions.

\subsection{Pruning Configuration}
\label{sec:pruning_config}

\paragraph{Sparsity and granularity.}
We apply 20\% structured sparsity by scoring and ranking both attention GQA groups (each comprising 1 KV head and 7 query heads) and MLP channel groups in a unified importance list; at 20\% sparsity, the global ranking preferentially removes MLP channel groups (200 of 784), with attention groups receiving higher importance scores and remaining intact.
The MLP intermediate dimension (18{,}944) is partitioned into 28 equal-sized groups of 676 channels each.
Structures are scored and ranked in a unified list across both component types, enabling cross-component pruning decisions.
At 20\% sparsity, 200 MLP channel groups are removed across 28 layers, totaling approximately 1.45 billion parameters (each group contributes $676 \times 3{,}584 \times 3 = 7{,}268{,}352$ parameters across gate, up, and down projections).
Both configurations remove 200 MLP channel groups at matched parameter budgets (${\sim}$1.45B parameters each), but differ in \emph{which} groups are selected---the central comparison of this work.

\paragraph{Importance scoring.}
For each structure $h$, we compute Taylor-expansion importance $I(h)$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:importance}) using activations and gradients collected on 512 randomly sampled chain-of-thought examples from the GSM8K training set.
For GQA groups, we aggregate importance across the constituent query heads and KV head (Section~\ref{sec:stage1}).

\paragraph{CKA-based functional overlap.}
We compute intra-layer linear CKA (Eq.~\ref{eq:cka}) between all pairs of GQA groups and between all pairs of MLP channel groups within each layer, using the same 512 calibration examples.
The functional overlap $F(h)$ for each structure is the mean pairwise CKA with its within-layer peers (Eq.~\ref{eq:overlap}).

\paragraph{Recovery-aware scoring.}
The final pruning score $S(h) = I(h) \times (1 - F(h))^\alpha$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}) combines importance and functional overlap with $\alpha{=}1.0$.
Structures with the lowest $S(h)$ are pruned first, subject to the per-layer capacity floor.

\paragraph{Per-layer capacity floor.}
To prevent representational collapse in individual layers, we enforce that each layer retains at least 40\% of its MLP channel groups (${\ge}12$ of 28 groups per layer).
During structure selection, candidates whose removal would violate this constraint are skipped in favor of the next lowest-scoring structure.
This constraint is necessary because unconstrained global ranking at 20\% sparsity concentrates pruning in layers with uniformly high functional overlap, collapsing those layers' capacity.

\paragraph{Baseline: Standard importance-only pruning.}
As the primary baseline, we apply the same Taylor-expansion importance scoring and global ranking but without the CKA overlap penalty ($\alpha{=}0$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:score}), following LLM-Pruner~\citep{llmpruner2023}.
The same per-layer capacity floor and sparsity target are applied, ensuring that the only difference between Standard and \rasp{} is the head selection criterion.

\subsection{Recovery Pipeline}
\label{sec:recovery_pipeline}

After pruning, we apply a two-stage recovery pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:stage3}) consisting of SFT warmup followed by GRPO-based RLVR.

\paragraph{Stage 3a: SFT warmup.}
Structured pruning destroys the model's ability to generate EOS tokens, causing unterminated repetitive outputs that prevent GRPO from computing rewards.
We restore generation competence with a brief SFT phase on 7{,}473 chain-of-thought examples (the full GSM8K training set), trained for 2 epochs (1{,}870 gradient steps) with teacher forcing.
This stage is minimal in duration (2 epochs, 37 minutes): its sole purpose is to restore EOS generation and basic format compliance, not to recover reasoning ability.

\paragraph{Stage 3b: GRPO recovery.}
With generation competence restored, we apply GRPO~\citep{shao2024grpo} on GSM8K training problems to recover reasoning ability.
The reward is binary: $r{=}1$ if the extracted final answer matches the ground truth, $r{=}0$ otherwise.
We use the following configuration: learning rate $1{\times}10^{-6}$, maximum completion length 1{,}024 tokens, 1{,}000 training samples, 1 epoch, KL penalty coefficient $\beta{=}0$, sampling temperature 0.7, and DAPO-style clipped loss.
Setting $\beta{=}0$ removes the KL divergence penalty against the reference policy, allowing the pruned model to freely explore new reasoning paths unconstrained by its pre-pruning behavior---a deliberate choice for post-pruning recovery where the reference policy's reasoning trajectories may no longer be achievable through the reduced architecture.

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\input{tables/table1_postprune}
\input{tables/table2_recovery}

\paragraph{Post-prune accuracy.}
Table~\ref{tab:postprune} reports accuracy immediately after pruning, before any recovery.
At 20\% sparsity, \rasp{} ($\alpha{=}1.0$) retains 29\% GSM8K accuracy compared to 1\% for Standard importance-only pruning ($N{=}100$, $p{<}0.0001$, Fisher's exact test).
Both configurations remove 200 MLP channel groups (of 784 total) at matched parameter budgets (${\sim}$1.45B parameters), with all attention GQA groups intact; the difference lies entirely in \emph{which} groups are selected.
Standard pruning removes the 200 lowest-importance groups, inadvertently discarding functionally unique structures whose computations cannot be compensated by retained peers.
\rasp{} instead removes important-but-redundant groups---those with high functional overlap ($F(h)$)---whose function can be absorbed by similar retained structures, while protecting functionally unique groups that would leave irrecoverable gaps.
The 28-percentage-point advantage demonstrates that recovery-aware selection preserves substantially more reasoning capacity at identical sparsity.

\paragraph{Recovery pipeline.}
Table~\ref{tab:recovery} tracks accuracy through the two-stage recovery pipeline.
After SFT warmup (Stage~3a), Standard-pruned models reach 64\% accuracy while \rasp{}-pruned models reach 57\% ($N{=}100$; the difference is not statistically significant at this sample size, $p \approx 0.32$, Fisher's exact test).
This reversal is consistent with SFT's recovery mechanism: teacher-forced trajectory imitation favors architectures where the most important individual structures are retained, regardless of functional uniqueness.
Standard pruning optimizes precisely for this criterion by protecting high-importance structures, making it better suited to SFT-based recovery.
The SFT stage restores EOS generation (99\% termination rate for both configurations, up from 0\%) and basic format compliance in 37 minutes, fulfilling its intended role as a minimal warmup rather than a reasoning recovery method.

[TBD: GRPO results. After GRPO recovery (Stage~3b), we expect the comparison to shift: \rasp{}'s preservation of functionally unique structures should provide a better substrate for RLVR's global exploration, potentially reversing the SFT-stage ordering.]

\paragraph{Recovery $\Delta$.}
[TBD: Recovery $\Delta$ analysis. Post-prune $\to$ post-GRPO gains for both configurations. The key question is whether \rasp{}'s 28-point post-prune advantage translates to a net advantage after the full pipeline, or whether Standard's SFT-stage lead persists through GRPO.]

\paragraph{Statistical significance.}
[TBD: Full evaluation on $N{=}1{,}319$ (complete GSM8K test set) with Fisher's exact test for pairwise comparisons at each pipeline stage. Preliminary results on $N{=}100$ are reported above; final significance testing awaits GRPO completion.]

\subsection{Ablation Studies}
\label{sec:ablation}

\input{tables/table3_alpha}

\paragraph{Effect of CKA balancing coefficient $\alpha$.}
The coefficient $\alpha$ in the recovery-aware score $S(h) = I(h) \times (1 - F(h))^\alpha$ controls how strongly functional overlap influences pruning decisions.
At $\alpha{=}0$, the overlap term vanishes and \rasp{} reduces to standard importance-only pruning; as $\alpha$ increases, structures with high functional overlap $F(h)$ receive progressively lower scores and are pruned preferentially.
Table~\ref{tab:alpha} reports post-prune accuracy across $\alpha \in \{0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0\}$ at fixed 20\% sparsity.
At $\alpha{=}1.0$ (our default), the model retains 29\% post-prune accuracy.
[TBD: Results for $\alpha \in \{0.0, 0.5, 2.0, 4.0\}$ and post-GRPO accuracy across all $\alpha$ values. We expect a non-monotonic relationship: moderate $\alpha$ improves post-GRPO recovery by protecting unique structures, while excessively large $\alpha$ over-weights overlap and ignores importance, degrading both post-prune and post-recovery accuracy.]

\paragraph{Per-layer capacity floor.}
[TBD: Ablation over the capacity floor threshold $\tau$, currently set to 40\% (each layer retains ${\ge}12$ of 28 MLP groups). Without this floor, unconstrained global ranking concentrates pruning in layers with uniformly high functional overlap, collapsing those layers' representational capacity. We plan to evaluate $\tau \in \{0\%, 20\%, 40\%, 60\%\}$ to quantify the floor's contribution.]

